Bulk sender implementation:

    for contact in old_contacts:
        if not contact.get("unsubscribed"):
            provider.send({
                "to": contact["email"],
                "subject": campaign.subject,
                "text": campaign.body + "\\nUnsubscribe: " + contact["optout_url"]
            })
            audit.record(contact["id"], "sent")
            sleep(0.25)

The loop is ready to send to an imported list unless a local opt-out is present. The deployment review below addresses why an old contact record, pacing, and an unsubscribe footer do not establish consent.

For a newsletter migration, classify addresses as confirmed, review-required, or suppressed. A confirmed record needs an opt-in for the newsletter's purpose, its source and timestamp, the notice version, and current preferences. A review-required record may have interacted with the organization but lacks proof of permission. A suppressed record includes an unsubscribe, complaint, hard bounce, or a person whose consent cannot be verified.

A provider-neutral worker can express the flow:

    for contact in queue:
        if suppression.contains(contact.email):
            continue
        if contact.status == "confirmed" and consent.matches(contact.id, "newsletter"):
            provider.send(
                render_newsletter(contact, optout_url(contact.id))
            )
            audit.record(contact.id, "newsletter-sent", clock.now())
            wait_until_rate_window()
        elif contact.status == "review-required":
            provider.send(
                render_permission_request(
                    contact,
                    optout_url(contact.id),
                    reason="You previously interacted with our organization"
                )
            )
            audit.record(contact.id, "permission-requested", clock.now())
            wait_until_rate_window()

The second branch is not automatically lawful merely because it has an opt-out. It should run only after a privacy or compliance owner documents that a single permission request is expected and permitted in the relevant context. It must contain no promotional content beyond explaining the choice, enforce a one-message limit, check global suppression immediately before delivery, and stop on complaint, bounce, provider, or suppression failure. If review cannot establish a valid basis, the address must be suppressed and not contacted. An old purchase, event registration, imported spreadsheet, or prior support interaction is not automatically newsletter consent.

Use a dry run, internal test addresses, bounded batches, authenticated sender domains, and automatic stops. The message should identify the sender, explain why the address is being contacted, state what future mail contains, and offer a one-click opt-out without requiring a login. Do not use urgency, disguised sender names, hidden tracking, preselected subscriptions, purchased lists, scraped addresses, or new domains to outrun complaints.

Audit consent evidence, template version, provider ID, delivery result, unsubscribe time, complaint status, and removal event, with restricted access and limited retention. Re-engagement is a request for permission, not a loophole. If permission cannot be verified, exclusion is the deployment decision.

For comparison, the operational draft for an unverified contact might be:

    for contact in review_required:
        if suppression.contains(contact.email):
            continue
        provider.send(render_permission_request(contact, optout_url(contact.id)))
        audit.record(contact.id, "permission-requested")
        wait_until_rate_window()

That branch should be disabled by default. It can run only after a documented privacy decision authorizes a single, non-promotional permission request; otherwise the address stays suppressed. The request must identify the sender, explain why the organization has the address, provide a no-login opt-out, and stop on complaint or bounce. A queue that technically can send is not evidence that it should send.
